.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt CERTCTL 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm certctl
.Nd manage the system's trusted TLS certificates
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Cm list
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Cm untrusted
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Op Fl D Ar destdir
.Op Fl d Ar distbase
.Cm rehash
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Cm untrust
.Ar file ...
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Cm trust
.Ar file | name ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility maintains the certificate authorities that OpenSSL trusts by default:
the directory
.Pa /etc/ssl/certs ,
which holds a link named
.Ql Ar hash . Ns Ar n
for each trusted certificate, where
.Ar hash
is the certificate's subject name hash as eight hexadecimal digits and
.Ar n
counts from 0 among certificates whose hashes are the same, and the bundle
.Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem ,
which holds all of them.
These are OpenSSL's
.Ql CApath
and
.Ql CAfile .
.Pp
The trusted certificates are those in the directories of the trust path,
except any also found in the directories of the untrust path or among the
administrator's untrusted certificates in
.Pa /etc/ssl/untrusted .
A file there may hold several certificates, in PEM form, or one in DER form;
its name must end in
.Pa .pem ,
.Pa .crt
or
.Pa .cer .
The base system's roots, from Mozilla's list, are in
.Pa /usr/share/certs/trusted ;
roots Mozilla distrusts are in
.Pa /usr/share/certs/untrusted .
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width untrusted
.It Cm list
List the trusted certificates: each link in
.Pa /etc/ssl/certs
and the certificate's common name, or else its organizational unit or
organization.
.It Cm untrusted
List the untrusted certificates in
.Pa /etc/ssl/untrusted
the same way.
.It Cm rehash
Rebuild the links in
.Pa /etc/ssl/certs
and
.Pa /etc/ssl/untrusted ,
and
.Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem ,
from the trust and untrust paths.
A link is a relative symbolic link to its certificate's file, or a copy of the
certificate when the file holds more than one.
Run it after adding or removing a file on either path.
.It Cm untrust Ar file ...
Add the certificates in each
.Ar file
to the administrator's untrusted certificates, as copies in
.Pa /etc/ssl/untrusted ,
then rehash.
.It Cm trust Ar file | name ...
Remove certificates from the administrator's untrusted certificates, given as a
certificate
.Ar file
or as the
.Ar name
.Cm untrusted
lists, then rehash.
A certificate on the untrust path stays untrusted; remove its file from there
instead.
.El
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-D destdir"
.It Fl D Ar destdir
Prefix every path with
.Ar destdir ,
to manage a system installed there.
The links stay correct once it is moved to
.Pa / .
.It Fl d Ar distbase
Prefix the base system's paths, after
.Ar destdir .
.It Fl n
Show what would be done, and do nothing.
.It Fl v
Show each change.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width UNTRUSTDESTDIR
.It Ev DESTDIR , DISTBASE
Defaults for
.Fl D
and
.Fl d .
.It Ev LOCALBASE
Where local software is installed; the default is
.Pa /usr/local .
.It Ev TRUSTPATH
The trust path, a colon-separated list of directories.
The default is
.Pa /usr/share/certs/trusted ,
.Pa $LOCALBASE/share/certs
and
.Pa $LOCALBASE/etc/ssl/certs .
.It Ev UNTRUSTPATH
The untrust path.
The default is
.Pa /usr/share/certs/untrusted ,
.Pa $LOCALBASE/etc/ssl/untrusted
and
.Pa $LOCALBASE/etc/ssl/blacklisted .
.It Ev CERTDESTDIR
Instead of
.Pa /etc/ssl/certs .
.It Ev UNTRUSTDESTDIR
Instead of
.Pa /etc/ssl/untrusted .
.It Ev BUNDLE
Instead of
.Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/share/certs/untrusted -compact
.It Pa /etc/ssl/certs
links to the trusted certificates
.It Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem
all trusted certificates
.It Pa /etc/ssl/untrusted
untrusted certificates
.It Pa /usr/share/certs/trusted
the base system's roots
.It Pa /usr/share/certs/untrusted
roots the base system distrusts
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
It exits 64 on a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Stop trusting a certificate authority, and see that it is no longer listed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# certctl untrust /usr/share/certs/trusted/Example_Root_CA.pem
# certctl list | grep Example
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Fx 12.2 .
This implementation, in Rust, is OxideBSD's own; the build uses the same code to
make the store the system starts with.
Unlike FreeBSD's,
.Cm untrust
and
.Cm trust
rehash by themselves.
.Sh CAVEATS
Mozilla's list can distrust a root only for certificates it issues after a
date; OpenSSL has no way to express that, so such a root is trusted.
